! INDEX INDICATORS IN PROGRAM ORDER -- reduction of C22-6578 Problem 4.7
! Method 2's DIAG1/DIAG2 loops (decks/problem_4_7_method2.strap): a
! progressive VFL instruction counting one index register, then an
! immediate index instruction counting another, then BZXCZ.  The branch
! must see the SECOND count's XCZ.  Both instructions set the index
! result indicators (execute()'s setIndexResultIndicatorsF), the
! progressive one when its look-ahead level executes, so the V-ICR must
! not run ahead of that level (StretchIBox's iboxHazard, setsIndexInd).
!
! The divide by one (which leaves the accumulator's 255 as it is) keeps
! the E-box busy, so the ST's level is still pending when the V-ICR is
! prepared -- the shape of 4.7's loops, where the previous pass's
! connective is still running.
!
! RESULT, hand-derived: $3 counts 4 to 0, so the loop runs four times and
! stores four bytes of ones into ANS from bit 0; $1, counting 16, never
! reaches zero.  ANS w0 = 0xFFFFFFFF00000000, w1 = 0.
!
! INJECTION, observed 2026-11-16: with the setsIndexInd/anyProgressive
! line of iboxHazard removed, the loop runs on $1's count -- sixteen
! passes, ANS w0 = w1 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 2099 clocks: FAIL ANS_w0,
! ANS_w1 and the cycle count, nothing else (ANS_w2 and the PC pass).  With
! it: all pass, 687 clocks.
! CYCLES 687 -> 685, 2026-11-17: the preparation floor (StretchCore's `ib`
! and rl_prepare's load clock; docs/atlas_oracle.md "Closing the
! residuals" item 7).  With that mechanism removed the run is 687 again;
! no value moved.

        SLC, 1000.0
START   LX, $1, XWA; LX, $3, XW3
        LI(BU,8), 255
LOOP    /(BU,64), ONE                   ! keep the E-box busy: 255 / 1
        ST(BU,8)(V+IC), 0.8($1)         ! count $1
        V-ICR, $3, 1                    ! count $3
        BZXCZ, LOOP                     ! until $3's count is zero
PASS    BEW, $
XWA     XW, ANS, 16, $
XW3     XW, 0, 4, XW3
ONE     DD(BU,64,8), 1
        SLC, 1210.0
ANS     DD(BU,64,8), 0
        DD(BU,64,8), 0
        DD(BU,64,8), 0
        END, START
